   "dialogue": {
    "scene": "Looking at a family photo",
    "lines": [
     {
      "who": "Arun",
      "od": "ଏ କିଏ?",
      "tr": "e kie?",
      "en": "Who's this?"
     },
     {
      "who": "Rina",
      "od": "ଏ ମୋ ବାପା, ଆଉ ଏ ମୋ ମାଆ।",
      "tr": "e mo bapa, au e mo maa.",
      "en": "This is my dad, and this is my mum."
     },
     {
      "who": "Arun",
      "od": "ଆଉ ଏ କିଏ? ତୁମ ଭାଇ?",
      "tr": "au e kie? tuma bhai?",
      "en": "And who's this? Your brother?"
     },
     {
      "who": "Rina",
      "od": "ହଁ, ଏ ମୋ ସାନ ଭାଇ ବାପି।",
      "tr": "han, e mo sana bhai Bapi.",
      "en": "Yes, this is my younger brother, Bapi."
     },
     {
      "who": "Arun",
      "od": "ତୁମର ଦିଦି ଅଛନ୍ତି?",
      "tr": "tumara didi achhanti?",
      "en": "Do you have an elder sister?"
     },
     {
      "who": "Rina",
      "od": "ନାହିଁ, ମୋର ଜଣେ ଭାଇନା ଅଛନ୍ତି।",
      "tr": "nahin, mora jane bhaina achhanti.",
      "en": "No, I have an elder brother."
     }
    ]
   },
   "tip": {
    "title": "Elder and younger",
    "text": "Odia always marks age: ବଡ଼ (bara) = elder, ସାନ (sana) = younger, so ବଡ଼ ଭାଇ, ସାନ ଭାଇ, ବଡ଼ ଭଉଣୀ, ସାନ ଭଉଣୀ. Elder siblings are not called by name — you say ଭାଇନା or ଦିଦି. For elders, the respectful plural verb is used: ମୋର ଜଣେ ଭାଇନା ଅଛନ୍ତି."
   }
